1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,000
They are protected beneath tons of earth and are the most challenging offensive targets

2
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
because they are the ultimate defensive hideouts.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Protecting civilians, soldiers, and our deadliest weapons.

4
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, Bunkers on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,720
From indomitable fortified structures

6
00:00:38,721 --> 00:00:43,900
to secret hideaways buried deep beneath the ground,

7
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:47,600
bunkers offer protection from one's enemies

8
00:00:47,601 --> 00:00:52,280
and the hope of a refuge in the face of unthinkable danger.

9
00:00:52,281 --> 00:01:03,080
One of the largest, most secret bunkers ever built lies nestled in a lush and remote pocket

10
00:01:03,081 --> 00:01:06,640
of the Allegheny Mountains, near White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia.

11
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:14,020
The luxurious Greenbrier Hotel and recreational facility is a five-hour drive from the nation's

12
00:01:14,021 --> 00:01:14,721
capital.

13
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:19,580
This 6,500-acre resort is a playground for the rich and famous.

14
00:01:19,581 --> 00:01:28,060
The 531-room main hotel is complemented by three golf courses and innumerable amenities.

15
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:34,780
But this old southern-style mansion belies a deep secret.

16
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:40,860
Behind this utility door, marked high voltage to keep out the curious,

17
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:45,500
lies the entrance to one of the largest, most secret bunkers of the Cold War.

18
00:01:45,501 --> 00:01:52,320
I'm standing at the wheel of a 25-ton blast door.

19
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:58,260
This is one of four entrances into the bunker itself.

20
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:00,820
We're standing at the west tunnel entrance.

21
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:07,080
This particular door, as well as the other three doors, all have to be manually operated.

22
00:02:07,081 --> 00:02:19,840
It is 10 by 18, 18 inches thick, and it swings freely, enclosing it to lock it into place.

23
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:29,900
This bunker was designed to protect not an individual or small group,

24
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:36,560
but every member of the United States Congress in the event of a nuclear strike.

25
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:44,300
Code named Greek Island, the facility was encased by up to three feet of steel-reinforced concrete,

26
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:47,780
with portions resting nearly 100 feet beneath the ground.

27
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:54,300
This bunker really was an amazing project in the sense that it was not just a big concrete box.

28
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:58,720
It was a site where a whole segment of the government come down.

29
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:01,720
They could live here, they could sleep here, they could eat here.

30
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:04,180
All their physical needs were taken care of.

31
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:11,100
Eighteen Spartan dormitories with a capacity of 60 individuals each

32
00:03:11,101 --> 00:03:12,880
comprised the core of the bunker,

33
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:17,840
a dining facility able to feed 400 at a time,

34
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:21,760
and chambers large enough to accommodate sessions

35
00:03:21,761 --> 00:03:25,760
by both the House of Representatives and Senate completed the complex.

36
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:31,240
Enough food and water were stored to sustain the lawmakers for 60 days.

37
00:03:31,241 --> 00:03:39,920
Meanwhile, 42,000 gallons of diesel fuel was available to feed three generators, providing power for over a month.

38
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:45,100
For government planners, this seemed a long enough period to ensure delivery of more supplies.

39
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:53,140
Built in 1958, the construction of the Greenbrier bunker was shrouded in secrecy.

40
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:59,160
So the challenge was, the government is asking the Greenbrier and its owners, the Chesapeake and Ohio Railway,

41
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:07,980
to build a huge 112,000 square foot steel reinforced concrete bunker right next to a major luxury resort hotel

42
00:04:07,981 --> 00:04:10,000
without anybody noticing what they're doing.

43
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:13,440
So how are we going to do this is the issue.

44
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:18,120
And they come up with a very clever idea, which is we're going to build it right in front of everybody's nose.

45
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:22,960
The plan called for the Greenbrier Hotel to build a new wing.

46
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:30,140
Work on the new building would mask construction of the mammoth subterranean bunker.

47
00:04:30,141 --> 00:04:35,340
Tons of excavated earth from beneath the hotel were quietly disposed of by building an additional

48
00:04:35,341 --> 00:04:37,380
nine-hole golf course.

49
00:04:37,381 --> 00:04:43,380
But the simultaneous construction of the hotel and bunker led to some interesting compromises.

50
00:04:43,381 --> 00:04:48,220
An unusual aspect to the secret bunker is that a portion of it, about a fifth of the

51
00:04:48,221 --> 00:04:51,460
square footage, was in fact available to Greenbrier guests.

52
00:04:51,461 --> 00:04:55,800
In fact, situations would arise where you'd be standing there and somebody would sort of say,

53
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:58,320
hey, I understand there's a secret bunker at the Greenbrier.

54
00:04:58,780 --> 00:05:00,600
And you say, no, there's no such thing.

55
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:04,280
And there you were, standing inside of this secret bunker.

56
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:09,160
The room behind me was used by many thousands of Greenbrier guests.

57
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:11,660
They never realized it was part of the bunker.

58
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:15,880
Hidden behind this secret panel is an 18-ton blast door.

59
00:05:15,881 --> 00:05:19,520
When closed, it seals off the bunker from the hotel.

60
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:28,640
Despite the government's best efforts, covert construction and hidden doors could not keep the bunker secret.

61
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:38,920
A Washington Post article in 1992 blew its cover, effectively destroying the facility as thoroughly as a nuclear bomb.

62
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:46,140
Given the accuracy and power of today's weapons, a bunker is only as good as its anonymity.

63
00:05:46,141 --> 00:05:49,280
the newly exposed Greenbrier was retired

64
00:05:49,281 --> 00:05:55,660
but most certainly not before another top secret congressional bunker was opened to take its place

65
00:05:55,661 --> 00:05:58,160
with secrecy at a premium

66
00:05:58,161 --> 00:06:01,080
it goes without saying that the best bunkers in the world

67
00:06:01,081 --> 00:06:03,000
are the ones we know nothing about

68
00:06:03,001 --> 00:06:08,880
the president of the United States is said to have access to a network of top secret bunkers

69
00:06:08,881 --> 00:06:12,620
probably located on or near military bases around the country

70
00:06:12,621 --> 00:06:20,460
In addition, the President relies on a sort of flying bunker, Air Force One.

71
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:27,840
Able to refuel in midair, the plane can stay aloft indefinitely or be flown to any number of secret hideouts.

72
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:35,520
Bunkers, of course, can protect us from the enemy, but they can also protect the enemy from us.

73
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:40,340
That's why building bunkers is only half of any country's defensive challenge.

74
00:06:41,340 --> 00:06:44,720
Learning how to smash through bunkers is equally important.

75
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:55,880
Beneath this desert landscape sits a state-of-the-art fortified defensive position, a military bunker.

76
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:03,940
Safely hidden from sight, the bunker is built of concrete and reinforced steel and protected by tons of earth.

77
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:07,820
But it is about to be utterly destroyed.

78
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:23,500
The GBU-28 bunker buster bomb has been specially designed to destroy hardened underground targets.

79
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:27,780
The GBU-28 is this weapon right here.

80
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:34,540
In the front, it's got a laser detector where we can spot a laser on the ground,

81
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:37,900
and this weapon will fly directly to that spot very accurately.

82
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:44,100
The heart of this bomb is a 5,000-pound warhead.

83
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:50,860
But it is the brains of the weapon that make it especially effective against bunkers.

84
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:55,720
A fuse is a little device used to set off this warhead,

85
00:07:56,080 --> 00:08:03,200
and what it'll enable this weapon to do is actually count the floors it's going through in an underground bunker.

86
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:06,600
So it can actually sense different stories,

87
00:08:06,601 --> 00:08:12,140
and we can have it go off in a precise room where a critical computer, radio transmitter,

88
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:15,500
maybe even weapons of mass destruction are located.

89
00:08:17,140 --> 00:08:23,760
Weapons such as the GBU-28 underscore a fact that bunker builders have struggled with since the dawn of the nuclear age.

90
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:30,220
The destructive power unleashed by today's weapons is far greater than any structure can withstand.

91
00:08:30,221 --> 00:08:37,700
But that has not stopped people from trying to build the perfect bunker.

92
00:08:39,660 --> 00:08:47,260
One of the most impressive bunkers in the world lies beneath 9,565-foot Cheyenne Mountain in Colorado Springs, Colorado.

93
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:54,780
Entombed nearly a third of a mile under this massive granite peak, sits the Cheyenne Mountain Operations Center.

94
00:08:54,781 --> 00:09:03,023
The reason that the United States established Cheyenne Mountain as a bunker was to create a central facility within which it could collect information initially about

95
00:09:03,024 --> 00:09:10,868
bomber attacks against the United States, hence its name, the North American Air Defense Command, NORAD, and later to warn the United States of an attack with

96
00:09:10,869 --> 00:09:14,940
ballistic missiles that presumably would have been launched from the Soviet Union.

97
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:23,460
Built in 1966, Cheyenne Mountain was one of the deepest and best protected military bunkers on the planet.

98
00:09:24,780 --> 00:09:31,780
The facility was specially designed to withstand a near hit by a nuclear device.

99
00:09:31,781 --> 00:09:37,240
Shielded by the mountain, the 15-building complex was shock isolated on hundreds of three-foot

100
00:09:37,241 --> 00:09:41,020
steel springs.

101
00:09:41,021 --> 00:09:45,580
In the event of an attack, these springs would provide a moving barrier between the facility

102
00:09:45,581 --> 00:09:49,580
and shifting earth.

103
00:09:49,581 --> 00:09:53,080
25-ton blast doors seal the four-and-a-half-acre facility,

104
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:57,660
protecting the military and civilian staff from the force and heat of a nuclear blast.

105
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:03,280
Nearly 1,200 people can survive on this mountain for up to three weeks.

106
00:10:04,920 --> 00:10:07,920
At a cost of $142.4 million,

107
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:12,480
the massive bunker jointly run by the United States and Canada

108
00:10:12,481 --> 00:10:15,760
is still active, protecting the personnel and equipment

109
00:10:15,761 --> 00:10:18,240
that scan the skies for enemy attacks.

110
00:10:19,580 --> 00:10:24,800
But few experts believe even this bunker could withstand a barrage from today's nuclear arsenal.

111
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:30,640
In the early days, nuclear weapons were not terribly accurate.

112
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:35,600
A place like Cheyenne Mountain, for example, with the NORAD headquarters,

113
00:10:35,601 --> 00:10:39,360
was reasonably safe because it was unlikely they could be directly hit.

114
00:10:39,660 --> 00:10:44,580
But today, with super-accurate missiles, there's virtually no place that's invulnerable.

115
00:10:44,581 --> 00:10:45,740
This is an Eastern company.

116
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:49,900
Not even a bunker in the middle of a mountain.

117
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:55,940
Next, cannons and forts face off,

118
00:10:56,940 --> 00:11:01,320
while high explosives bring armies to new lows during World War I.

119
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:09,680
The first GBU-28 Bunker Buster bombs were made from used cannon barrels.

120
00:11:10,820 --> 00:11:12,980
Bunkers will return on Modern Marvels.

121
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:21,580
Bunkers are relatively recent creations on the military fortification family tree.

122
00:11:21,581 --> 00:11:27,580
But the story of defensive structures goes back to ancient times and the beginnings of war.

123
00:11:27,581 --> 00:11:32,580
Fortifications have existed as long as people have built buildings.

124
00:11:32,581 --> 00:11:38,580
From the very earliest ages, there was a need to defend against some sort of hostile threat.

125
00:11:38,581 --> 00:11:39,281
threat.

126
00:11:39,282 --> 00:11:44,960
And as technology advanced, the need to improve those defenses to meet that technology

127
00:11:44,961 --> 00:11:46,043
also increased.

128
00:11:46,044 --> 00:11:51,600
For thousands of years, fortified positions had the upper hand during battle.

129
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:58,500
Ancient siege machines were so ineffective against citadels and walled cities that attacking

130
00:11:58,501 --> 00:12:01,064
armies often resorted to treachery.

131
00:12:01,065 --> 00:12:05,460
During the legendary Trojan War, the Greeks spent nine years

132
00:12:05,461 --> 00:12:07,395
trying to breach the walls of Troy.

133
00:12:07,396 --> 00:12:10,380
They finally succeeded after convincing the Trojans to

134
00:12:10,381 --> 00:12:13,119
accept a giant gift horse made of wood.

135
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:16,700
Greek soldiers hidden inside the horse waited until

136
00:12:16,701 --> 00:12:20,656
they were safely through the city gates to mount their attack.

137
00:12:20,657 --> 00:12:22,060
The clandestine Greeks

138
00:12:22,061 --> 00:12:25,991
bear testament to the near invulnerability of early strongholds.

139
00:12:25,992 --> 00:12:27,220
From primitive forts

140
00:12:27,221 --> 00:12:33,280
of earth and rock to medieval castles of carved stone, these fortifications enabled a relatively

141
00:12:33,281 --> 00:12:37,904
small number of defenders to hold off armies many times their size.

142
00:12:37,905 --> 00:12:39,560
The first real challenge

143
00:12:39,561 --> 00:12:45,100
to defensive superiority came with the invention of the cannon, a powerful yet relatively mobile

144
00:12:45,101 --> 00:12:46,246
siege weapon.

145
00:12:46,247 --> 00:12:53,120
First used in the 14th century, projectiles fired from cannons forced builders

146
00:12:53,121 --> 00:12:56,500
to construct thicker walls to safeguard the structures within.

147
00:12:57,940 --> 00:13:02,780
When artillery first came along, the first attempt that the military engineers tried

148
00:13:02,781 --> 00:13:06,593
to do was to make their walls thicker and stronger, but that didn't work.

149
00:13:06,594 --> 00:13:07,294
As artillery

150
00:13:07,295 --> 00:13:11,400
got better, it was able to knock down the stone walls no matter how thick they made them.

151
00:13:15,080 --> 00:13:19,040
Military engineers responded by sinking fortress walls into the ground,

152
00:13:19,840 --> 00:13:21,920
using the earth to absorb a shell's impact.

153
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:29,060
The new defensive technique, coupled with the relative inaccuracy of early artillery,

154
00:13:29,061 --> 00:13:31,820
shifted the advantage back to the defender,

155
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:34,720
where it remained well into the 19th century.

156
00:13:36,820 --> 00:13:39,380
We've reached a stage where the technology of war

157
00:13:39,381 --> 00:13:42,540
makes fixed, known facilities highly vulnerable.

158
00:13:43,020 --> 00:13:44,380
Once you have accurate artillery,

159
00:13:44,680 --> 00:13:46,400
you have the ability to destroy a facility.

160
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:47,860
It might take you days or weeks,

161
00:13:48,300 --> 00:13:52,260
but very few things can withstand long-term assault with artillery.

162
00:13:54,520 --> 00:13:58,200
The American Civil War saw the first widespread use

163
00:13:58,201 --> 00:13:59,600
of these more accurate weapons.

164
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:04,680
In 1862, just two years after the start of hostilities,

165
00:14:05,460 --> 00:14:09,280
the Confederate held Fort Pulaski on Coxsburg Island in Savannah, Georgia,

166
00:14:09,880 --> 00:14:13,440
bore the brunt of an attack using newly developed rifle-barreled cannons.

167
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:18,280
Union shells fired from these weapons were stunningly accurate.

168
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:25,520
Fort Pulaski's 7 1⁄2-foot-thick walls were pummeled by artillery fired more than a mile away.

169
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:34,900
The dominance of ramped earth and stone walls was quickly coming to an end.

170
00:14:37,380 --> 00:14:42,780
The introduction of high explosives at the end of the 19th century made forts even more obsolete.

171
00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:47,820
High explosives were capable of doing a great amount of damage,

172
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:51,600
more than any masonry or earth wall could sustain.

173
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:54,760
So there was a need to develop a new technology to deal with that.

174
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,880
And the technology that they came up with was reinforced concrete.

175
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:05,200
At the turn of the 20th century, both France and Germany began to use the new technology

176
00:15:05,201 --> 00:15:07,091
to strengthen existing forts.

177
00:15:07,092 --> 00:15:11,200
Neither country realized that just 15 years later, the hardened

178
00:15:11,201 --> 00:15:17,232
structures would be at the center of the greatest battles of the First World War.

179
00:15:17,233 --> 00:15:18,200
Germans built

180
00:15:18,201 --> 00:15:23,200
the first true military bunkers and incorporated them into their trench lines during World War I.

181
00:15:23,201 --> 00:15:28,660
These relatively small structures made of steel and reinforced concrete

182
00:15:28,661 --> 00:15:31,920
defended German troops, command centers and weapons

183
00:15:31,921 --> 00:15:36,160
against the nearly 5 million tons of explosives fired by the Allies.

184
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:45,600
Here in the French countryside lay the remnants of a World War I concrete bunker.

185
00:15:47,940 --> 00:15:50,880
Less than 120 kilometers north of Paris,

186
00:15:50,881 --> 00:15:55,800
this structure was once part of a 250-mile trench and bunker complex

187
00:15:55,801 --> 00:15:58,040
comprising the famed Western Front.

188
00:15:59,340 --> 00:16:02,280
Today, the bunker's rusting steel reinforcing rods

189
00:16:02,281 --> 00:16:04,460
protrude from the crumbling concrete shell

190
00:16:04,461 --> 00:16:09,500
as it marks the boundary between a bean patch and a field of wheat.

191
00:16:13,640 --> 00:16:18,000
During the war, machine gunners inside these bunkers were blinded by smoke

192
00:16:18,001 --> 00:16:22,000
and deafened by the sound of their weapons reverberating off the bare walls.

193
00:16:23,660 --> 00:16:26,580
But being inside the bunker was far preferable to being outside.

194
00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:31,840
Firing through slits, known as embrasures,

195
00:16:32,620 --> 00:16:35,420
bunker gunners took a terrible toll in Allied lives.

196
00:16:36,200 --> 00:16:38,680
So hated were the machine gunners that upon surrendering,

197
00:16:39,220 --> 00:16:41,340
they were often executed by vengeful soldiers.

198
00:16:42,340 --> 00:16:45,200
Still, the bunkers were remarkably successful.

199
00:16:45,201 --> 00:16:54,240
One of the reasons that the bunker concept really took hold was the realization that the old-fashioned fort was really a case of putting all of your eggs in one basket.

200
00:16:54,860 --> 00:16:56,620
A fort was considered to be a target.

201
00:16:57,900 --> 00:17:01,820
And damage to one portion could potentially damage another portion.

202
00:17:02,220 --> 00:17:04,880
So you wanted to disperse your defense as much as possible.

203
00:17:05,400 --> 00:17:08,320
That led to putting everything in isolated bunkers.

204
00:17:10,300 --> 00:17:14,660
France and Germany each suffered nearly a million and a half casualties in the Great War.

205
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:22,660
The catastrophic losses prompted both countries to create bunker and defensive line fortifications on an unprecedented scale.

206
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:30,500
The French began construction on the Maginot Line in 1929,

207
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:35,360
a 200-mile defensive barrier that they believed would keep Germany at bay.

208
00:17:36,380 --> 00:17:41,700
Stretching over much of the northern and eastern borders of France and incorporating many natural barriers,

209
00:17:42,420 --> 00:17:45,580
The mammoth structure may be the greatest bunker complex ever built.

210
00:17:46,640 --> 00:17:51,160
The Maginot line used the theory of a thin, tough skin.

211
00:17:51,940 --> 00:17:55,400
A very narrow, but very strong line of fortifications

212
00:17:55,401 --> 00:17:57,580
that was designed to repulse the enemy.

213
00:17:58,980 --> 00:18:02,080
The fortification consisted of a line of strong,

214
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:05,060
mutually supporting, completely self-sufficient bunkers.

215
00:18:06,540 --> 00:18:09,140
Armed with machine guns and anti-tank weapons,

216
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,760
Much of the line bristled with artillery.

217
00:18:14,360 --> 00:18:18,900
Some of the structures were connected via tunnel to a subterranean support complex,

218
00:18:19,500 --> 00:18:24,180
containing a command post, living quarters, storage facilities, and power plant.

219
00:18:25,420 --> 00:18:28,240
In short, everything necessary to make them fully independent.

220
00:18:29,140 --> 00:18:34,860
The largest structures within the line utilized integrated rail systems to move men and ammunition.

221
00:18:34,861 --> 00:18:38,680
taken as a whole

222
00:18:38,681 --> 00:18:41,160
the Maginot Line was a formidable obstacle

223
00:18:41,161 --> 00:18:42,620
to any hostile advance

224
00:18:42,621 --> 00:18:45,540
but it would not fulfill

225
00:18:45,541 --> 00:18:47,720
the French desire to keep the Germans out

226
00:18:47,721 --> 00:18:51,420
in 1940 soon after

227
00:18:51,421 --> 00:18:52,780
the outbreak of World War II

228
00:18:52,781 --> 00:18:55,640
Hitler's forces completely circumvented

229
00:18:55,641 --> 00:18:56,480
the Maginot Line

230
00:18:56,481 --> 00:18:59,140
by first invading Luxembourg and Belgium

231
00:18:59,141 --> 00:19:01,580
to the north and then descending into France

232
00:19:01,581 --> 00:19:02,800
between the wars

233
00:19:02,801 --> 00:19:06,200
the Germans in particular developed a new approach to warfare.

234
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:11,240
They'd seen what happened in trench warfare and they didn't want to repeat it.

235
00:19:11,440 --> 00:19:13,920
And they looked at ways to avoid repeating that.

236
00:19:15,940 --> 00:19:20,340
The French, on the other hand, were basically still ready to fight the First World War.

237
00:19:21,080 --> 00:19:22,920
They weren't prepared for what the Germans did.

238
00:19:23,660 --> 00:19:26,500
And when the Germans went round the Maginot Line, they had no counter.

239
00:19:26,501 --> 00:19:30,960
after overrunning most of France

240
00:19:30,961 --> 00:19:34,440
the Germans circled back to finally take on the Maginot line

241
00:19:34,441 --> 00:19:38,020
but even when attacked from behind

242
00:19:38,021 --> 00:19:41,720
the French fortifications were exceptionally difficult to destroy

243
00:19:41,721 --> 00:19:47,300
the Germans unleashed heavy artillery

244
00:19:47,301 --> 00:19:48,640
fired from a distance

245
00:19:48,641 --> 00:19:52,760
but bringing forward special guns and zeroing in on the target

246
00:19:52,761 --> 00:19:53,860
took precious time

247
00:19:53,861 --> 00:20:07,861
For the lightning-fast German war machine, the best offense against bunkers was infantry soldiers.

248
00:20:13,820 --> 00:20:19,180
The many different types of bunkers in the Maginot Line meant that no one textbook maneuver worked.

249
00:20:19,181 --> 00:20:27,020
most plans involved first blinding the bunkers with flamethrowers

250
00:20:27,021 --> 00:20:35,200
then moving in close to plant an explosive device known as a satchel charge

251
00:20:35,201 --> 00:20:49,201
Though the bunkers were clearly vulnerable, they had proven their effectiveness.

252
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:55,680
So much so that while the Germans fought to crush the remaining Maginot bunkers,

253
00:20:55,681 --> 00:21:00,300
they also built similar concrete structures on the western seaboard of France

254
00:21:00,301 --> 00:21:02,980
to protect their newly conquered territory.

255
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:12,700
But like the bunkers of the Maginot Line, the German fortifications would prolong the

256
00:21:12,701 --> 00:21:20,200
battle but ultimately lose the war.

257
00:21:20,201 --> 00:21:25,760
Next, the weapons of the Second World War force engineers to construct some of the greatest

258
00:21:25,761 --> 00:21:29,720
bunkers ever built.

259
00:21:29,721 --> 00:21:35,440
German machine gun bunkers during World War I were referred to as pill boxes by English troops

260
00:21:35,441 --> 00:21:38,940
because they resembled a pharmacist's pill container.

261
00:21:38,941 --> 00:21:44,880
Bunkers will return on Modern Marvels.

262
00:21:44,881 --> 00:21:54,200
German submarines were the bane of allied ships during the Second World War.

263
00:21:54,201 --> 00:21:57,000
Patrolling the waters of the North Atlantic,

264
00:21:57,001 --> 00:22:01,100
the submarine stopped American supplies and troops from reaching European ports.

265
00:22:02,120 --> 00:22:05,380
When submerged, these stealthy hunters were almost unstoppable.

266
00:22:06,200 --> 00:22:08,160
But above water, they were nearly helpless,

267
00:22:08,860 --> 00:22:12,560
particularly vulnerable when taking on supplies or being repaired.

268
00:22:15,640 --> 00:22:20,920
Building a bunker to protect a single 250-foot submarine would be a major undertaking.

269
00:22:22,500 --> 00:22:25,500
But on the northeastern shores of occupied France,

270
00:22:26,260 --> 00:22:32,140
Germany built a submarine bunker complex to house not one, but up to 24 of their U-boats.

271
00:22:33,200 --> 00:22:38,100
Three mammoth block structures comprise the submarine complex in the seaside village of Lorient.

272
00:22:40,180 --> 00:22:45,480
Inside these bunkers lay a small city, able to house and protect the submarines,

273
00:22:46,020 --> 00:22:52,880
as well as perform repairs and store tons of ammunition, torpedoes, and fuel to supply the underwater killers.

274
00:22:55,500 --> 00:23:00,020
To build this phenomenal structure, they obviously needed a lot of workers.

275
00:23:01,240 --> 00:23:05,620
Nearly 15,000 prisoners labored for two years to build a base.

276
00:23:08,540 --> 00:23:11,900
Most of the labor went into constructing the roofs of the buildings.

277
00:23:14,660 --> 00:23:21,340
The bunker's ceilings were built of steel-reinforced concrete over 10 feet thick to protect against enemy bombs.

278
00:23:22,120 --> 00:23:25,000
The third and largest building has two roofs.

279
00:23:25,500 --> 00:23:27,500
making it nearly impenetrable.

280
00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:34,500
The first ceiling measures 3.5 meters thick

281
00:23:34,501 --> 00:23:36,500
and covers the entire building.

282
00:23:36,501 --> 00:23:40,500
Then there is an airspace, on top of which there is a second roof

283
00:23:40,501 --> 00:23:44,500
which covers approximately two-thirds of the bunker.

284
00:23:45,500 --> 00:23:47,500
The roofs were so well-built,

285
00:23:47,501 --> 00:23:51,500
they forced the British to develop special six-ton bunker buster bombs,

286
00:23:51,501 --> 00:23:53,500
called tall boys.

287
00:23:55,500 --> 00:24:00,040
On August 6, 1944, the Royal Air Force scored a direct hit.

288
00:24:01,240 --> 00:24:05,700
This 15-foot-wide crater on top of the roof is the result of the monstrous blast.

289
00:24:08,580 --> 00:24:13,460
But despite the tremendous explosion, the weapon was only able to penetrate a few feet.

290
00:24:14,380 --> 00:24:19,520
The roof was so thick, nearly 20 feet of solid reinforced concrete remained intact

291
00:24:19,521 --> 00:24:21,300
to protect the facility below.

292
00:24:21,301 --> 00:24:26,300
The Tallboy at least managed to land on the roof.

293
00:24:26,301 --> 00:24:31,300
Most other bombs earmarked for the bunker and the planes carrying them never came close.

294
00:24:31,301 --> 00:24:41,300
Over the course of the war, nearly 20,000 German soldiers were assigned to anti-aircraft batteries atop the bunker complex and throughout the surrounding city.

295
00:24:44,300 --> 00:24:48,300
Successful attacks by Allied pilots were nearly impossible.

296
00:24:48,301 --> 00:24:52,860
aerial frontal attacks on the bunker were equally difficult

297
00:24:52,861 --> 00:24:58,440
many of the submarine pens have water level blast doors that were lowered at

298
00:24:58,441 --> 00:24:59,700
the first sign of trouble

299
00:24:59,701 --> 00:25:05,040
the barrier would stop torpedoes and specially designed skipping bombs from

300
00:25:05,041 --> 00:25:05,741
literally

301
00:25:05,742 --> 00:25:10,240
entering through the front door

302
00:25:10,241 --> 00:25:13,620
for additional protection the German scuttled two ships in the harbor

303
00:25:13,621 --> 00:25:17,800
200 yards from the bunkers entrance the sunken ships provided

304
00:25:17,801 --> 00:25:23,200
an additional barrier to defend against torpedo attacks.

305
00:25:23,201 --> 00:25:25,960
The bunkers were impervious to nearly every weapon

306
00:25:25,961 --> 00:25:28,560
in the Allied arsenal.

307
00:25:28,561 --> 00:25:30,520
Still, they were vulnerable.

308
00:25:34,480 --> 00:25:37,120
The Lorient bunkers depended upon a steady stream

309
00:25:37,121 --> 00:25:39,980
of goods and materials to operate properly.

310
00:25:39,981 --> 00:25:42,580
Once the Allies realized they could not destroy the bunkers

311
00:25:42,581 --> 00:25:46,560
outright, they turned to cutting the supply lines.

312
00:25:46,561 --> 00:25:51,580
Unfortunately for the French, the bunker's supply lines ran directly through the city of Lorient.

313
00:25:55,420 --> 00:26:02,580
60,000 bombs fell on the city, and only 70 buildings were left standing in the town center after they were.

314
00:26:03,180 --> 00:26:04,900
The whole city had to be rebuilt.

315
00:26:05,460 --> 00:26:10,880
Fortunately, the Lorient population was completely evacuated before the bombing started.

316
00:26:11,380 --> 00:26:16,220
So on a human level, there were a few victims, but on a material level, we lost everything.

317
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:23,560
Lorient suffered the same devastating fate as many European cities during the war.

318
00:26:23,561 --> 00:26:28,560
But unlike Lorient, other cities were rarely able to evacuate beforehand.

319
00:26:28,561 --> 00:26:34,560
In London, German bombers showered the city with high explosives and incendiary bombs.

320
00:26:34,561 --> 00:26:37,560
The citizens sought refuge underground.

321
00:26:37,561 --> 00:26:41,560
Bunkers and hastily converted subway tunnel shelters

322
00:26:41,561 --> 00:26:44,560
protected the people while the city above them burned.

323
00:26:44,561 --> 00:26:49,560
The bombs that were raining down on London at that time, and indeed not just London,

324
00:26:49,561 --> 00:26:53,560
but practically every major city in the UK, were doing incredible damage.

325
00:26:53,561 --> 00:27:00,560
A stick of bombs could devastate a whole area, literally flatten buildings, destroy streets,

326
00:27:00,561 --> 00:27:05,560
destroy people's way of life. And you might go down to an underground shelter,

327
00:27:05,561 --> 00:27:11,560
maybe a tube shelter, or even just a street shelter, and come out and find your whole street

328
00:27:11,561 --> 00:27:12,960
it literally just disappeared.

329
00:27:15,480 --> 00:27:17,900
Disappearing was not an option for the British government,

330
00:27:18,320 --> 00:27:20,800
and in particular, Prime Minister Winston Churchill,

331
00:27:21,260 --> 00:27:22,840
who refused to leave London,

332
00:27:23,140 --> 00:27:25,600
worried that his absence would demoralize the British people.

333
00:27:27,560 --> 00:27:31,540
But where could high-level British officials take refuge as the bombs fell?

334
00:27:33,580 --> 00:27:35,380
The British addressed that question

335
00:27:35,381 --> 00:27:39,280
by building a top-secret, high-tech command bunker in the center of London.

336
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:51,294
Converted from a series of small basement storerooms in 1938, the Cabinet War Room served as the underground nerve center from which Churchill and his War Cabinet led

337
00:27:51,295 --> 00:27:51,995
the fight.

338
00:27:54,060 --> 00:28:01,160
The configuration of the Cabinet War Rooms in 1940 was about 21 rooms making up about 1,000 square meters.

339
00:28:01,940 --> 00:28:11,340
But when it extended in 1941 to 1945, it occupied somewhere in the region of about 100 rooms and was literally three times as big.

340
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:24,600
Located under the present-day Treasury Building, the bunker is within walking distance of both Parliament and Number 10 Downing Street.

341
00:28:27,600 --> 00:28:31,700
But it was chosen less for its size and location than for its strength.

342
00:28:32,200 --> 00:28:34,720
The building had a steel frame in it.

343
00:28:35,240 --> 00:28:39,900
Steel frame building technology was relatively new or even revolutionary at that time.

344
00:28:39,901 --> 00:28:45,920
It wouldn't necessarily stop a bomb, but it would hopefully withstand better if a bomb ever hit it.

345
00:28:48,140 --> 00:28:55,160
Unconcerned with aesthetics, British builders left these exposed steel girders visible in nearly every major room of the bunker.

346
00:28:56,940 --> 00:29:02,460
Oak joists shored up the smaller rooms and kept the ceiling from crashing down during bombing raids.

347
00:29:02,461 --> 00:29:07,820
I think the planners knew when it became a headquarters building

348
00:29:07,821 --> 00:29:10,460
that it wasn't, properly speaking, bomb-proof.

349
00:29:10,580 --> 00:29:14,860
But it was only at the end of 1940, so well into the bombing raids of the Blitz,

350
00:29:14,940 --> 00:29:16,700
which started in September that year,

351
00:29:17,140 --> 00:29:20,740
that they finally began to install proper bomb-proofing.

352
00:29:21,220 --> 00:29:24,760
And what they put in was a layer of reinforced concrete,

353
00:29:24,920 --> 00:29:26,800
and quite the strongest concrete you can imagine,

354
00:29:27,620 --> 00:29:30,260
varying in thickness between about 5 and 10 feet,

355
00:29:30,261 --> 00:29:31,500
depending on what it was covering.

356
00:29:32,460 --> 00:29:38,640
This cutaway in the ceiling reveals the thickness of the concrete as it rests upon the steel

357
00:29:38,641 --> 00:29:40,640
girder subframe.

358
00:29:40,641 --> 00:29:47,040
But it was not just the strength of this structure that made it such an effective command bunker.

359
00:29:47,041 --> 00:29:51,360
Behind this unassuming bathroom door lies one of the greatest technological secrets

360
00:29:51,361 --> 00:29:53,620
of the entire war.

361
00:29:53,621 --> 00:29:59,460
The war rooms were really Churchill's high-tech basement headquarters and one of the highest

362
00:29:59,461 --> 00:30:04,360
high-tech items in it, I suppose, was the so-called transatlantic telephone.

363
00:30:04,361 --> 00:30:05,520
And it allowed the

364
00:30:05,521 --> 00:30:08,708
Prime Minister to talk to the President in complete secrecy.

365
00:30:08,709 --> 00:30:10,780
And it was, in effect, really the first

366
00:30:10,781 --> 00:30:12,306
direct secret hotline.

367
00:30:12,307 --> 00:30:17,160
So it was behind this humble door marked with a very basic toilet lock

368
00:30:17,161 --> 00:30:21,580
that some of the most secret and high-level conversations of the war were taking place.

369
00:30:23,420 --> 00:30:28,040
But not every secret associated with the Cabinet war rooms was so diligently concealed.

370
00:30:28,041 --> 00:30:35,040
The cabinet war room's code name, rather ironically, was Number Two Stories Gate, which was its address.

371
00:30:35,041 --> 00:30:40,040
So I think if the Germans ever did find out about it, they had a pretty fair idea of where it might be.

372
00:30:40,041 --> 00:30:46,040
The Germans expected Churchill to be rather deeper and rather further away from his normal premises

373
00:30:46,041 --> 00:30:51,040
than literally ten feet underground, a hundred yards from his official residence.

374
00:30:51,041 --> 00:30:57,400
On the other side of the war, Adolf Hitler too sought shelter in underground bunkers.

375
00:30:59,400 --> 00:31:03,380
These bunkers were the backdrops for some of the most dramatic events in the war.

376
00:31:05,040 --> 00:31:12,460
The Fuhrer survived an assassination attempt after disillusioned German officers placed a bomb in his Wolfschanze bunker complex,

377
00:31:12,980 --> 00:31:15,540
which was located on the border between Poland and Germany.

378
00:31:16,480 --> 00:31:22,200
But the most infamous bunker in the world was constructed under the Chancellery Building in the heart of Berlin.

379
00:31:23,140 --> 00:31:27,020
The reason that bunker survived, and it survived until the very end of the war,

380
00:31:27,140 --> 00:31:31,460
when both Soviet and later American and other troops went in to investigate that facility,

381
00:31:31,900 --> 00:31:37,360
it survived because we as the Allies did not have weapons that were sufficiently accurate to destroy that bunker.

382
00:31:37,780 --> 00:31:41,580
What we were doing is launching aircraft and conducting raids against that bunker,

383
00:31:41,581 --> 00:31:46,420
but bombs in World War II had accuracies measured in hundreds if not thousands of meters.

384
00:31:46,980 --> 00:31:50,400
And to destroy a hardened, deep underground facility or bunker,

385
00:31:50,700 --> 00:31:55,660
you have to have weapons that land directly on that facility, otherwise it'll survive forever.

386
00:31:57,740 --> 00:32:01,360
Though the structure survived the onslaught, Hitler did not,

387
00:32:01,820 --> 00:32:05,440
taking his own life inside the bunker on April 30, 1945.

388
00:32:05,441 --> 00:32:15,140
With the defeat of Germany, America turned its full attention to the South Pacific and Japan.

389
00:32:16,240 --> 00:32:19,960
The death knell for the island nation arrived August 6, 1945.

390
00:32:24,280 --> 00:32:28,260
The atomic blast on Hiroshima killed nearly 100,000 people

391
00:32:28,261 --> 00:32:31,620
and forever changed the landscape of peace and war.

392
00:32:35,440 --> 00:32:39,300
Bunkers seemed pointless against the awesome power of a nuclear blast.

393
00:32:39,920 --> 00:32:46,480
Yet the terrible weapon would lead to a bunker-building explosion, the likes of which the world had never seen.

394
00:32:48,600 --> 00:32:55,620
Next, frightened Americans dig in deep, while the Air Force builds bunkers to protect the deadliest of weapons.

395
00:32:55,621 --> 00:33:06,260
Winston Churchill's cabinet war rooms bunker had many gadgets, including one of the first electric cigar lighters.

396
00:33:07,640 --> 00:33:09,780
Bunkers will return on Modern Marvels.

397
00:33:09,781 --> 00:33:20,780
With the onset of the Cold War

398
00:33:20,781 --> 00:33:23,400
and the sudden possibility of nuclear attack

399
00:33:23,401 --> 00:33:26,360
paranoia gripped the American public

400
00:33:26,361 --> 00:33:28,180
You might be out playing at home

401
00:33:28,181 --> 00:33:31,540
Stop, drop and cover became the survivalist mantra

402
00:33:31,541 --> 00:33:32,860
for an entire generation

403
00:33:32,861 --> 00:33:36,340
The United States government did its best

404
00:33:36,341 --> 00:33:37,760
to allay the nation's fears

405
00:33:37,761 --> 00:33:43,760
When danger threatened him, he never got hurt. He knew just what to do.

406
00:33:44,120 --> 00:33:50,320
Be sure and remember what Bert the Turtle just did, friends, because every one of us must remember to do the same thing.

407
00:33:50,820 --> 00:33:52,500
That's what this film is all about.

408
00:33:53,120 --> 00:34:00,660
I think in retrospect, as we look back on that time, I think many concluded that the prospects for survival would have been very dim indeed, very dismal indeed.

409
00:34:01,280 --> 00:34:07,360
But this was reflective of a time in which people deeply feared that their existence was on the line.

410
00:34:07,361 --> 00:34:09,660
and bunkers were seen as the solution.

411
00:34:11,080 --> 00:34:14,080
Despite the government's best efforts to provide protection,

412
00:34:14,800 --> 00:34:17,680
many Americans decided to take matters into their own hands.

413
00:34:24,160 --> 00:34:25,500
Backyard bomb shelters.

414
00:34:26,080 --> 00:34:28,720
The bunker for the man in the street became the rage.

415
00:34:29,320 --> 00:34:31,660
From basic store-bought models for less than $100

416
00:34:31,661 --> 00:34:35,400
to specially built structures that cost as much as a house.

417
00:34:36,220 --> 00:34:36,920
Hey!

418
00:34:37,360 --> 00:34:38,540
Isn't this nice?

419
00:34:39,700 --> 00:34:44,100
Well, Sir Ruth and I certainly can live in here very comfortably for at least two weeks.

420
00:34:45,420 --> 00:34:48,380
And, you know, this room can be put to other uses as well.

421
00:34:48,900 --> 00:34:52,180
Yes, Walt. You could use this as an extra bedroom for company.

422
00:34:52,800 --> 00:34:56,600
As the United States came out of the Second World War, we had massive armies,

423
00:34:56,780 --> 00:34:59,240
we had developed nuclear weapons, we had crushed two enemies.

424
00:35:00,160 --> 00:35:04,360
Contrast that with the condition in the United States beginning in the 1950s

425
00:35:04,361 --> 00:35:07,280
when the Soviet Union in 1949 detonated a nuclear weapon.

426
00:35:07,540 --> 00:35:11,520
We now, for the first time in our history, faced absolute vulnerability.

427
00:35:12,220 --> 00:35:16,240
We never before had faced a time in which our survival, our existence,

428
00:35:16,800 --> 00:35:18,980
could be eradicated on the order of 30 minutes.

429
00:35:19,440 --> 00:35:22,180
And the reaction was to build bunkers throughout American society,

430
00:35:22,460 --> 00:35:24,480
both on the governmental level and the military level,

431
00:35:24,481 --> 00:35:28,840
as well as bunkers throughout American society, in many cases within residences.

432
00:35:28,841 --> 00:35:37,120
wall street analysts in the 1950s projected the bomb shelter business as a potential 20 billion

433
00:35:37,121 --> 00:35:43,880
dollar industry but this was a quiet building boom the construction and subsequent storage of food

434
00:35:43,881 --> 00:35:49,620
water and supplies and underground shelters was performed secretly not from fear of the russians

435
00:35:49,621 --> 00:35:54,580
but from fear of one's own neighbors fighting for cover during a missile attack

436
00:35:54,581 --> 00:36:03,400
Ironically, the best Cold War bunkers did not protect people from nuclear weapons, but

437
00:36:03,401 --> 00:36:09,900
instead protected the nuclear weapons themselves.

438
00:36:09,901 --> 00:36:15,400
Buried deep in the desert flatlands outside of Tucson, Arizona, sits one of the most advanced

439
00:36:15,401 --> 00:36:23,040
and potentially deadly bunkers ever built.

440
00:36:23,041 --> 00:36:28,000
The facility and others like it were designed to protect and, if necessary, launch America's

441
00:36:28,001 --> 00:36:33,220
largest intercontinental ballistic missiles, the Titan IIs.

442
00:36:33,221 --> 00:36:37,420
Billed as a retaliatory weapon, the missile could be launched in less than 10 minutes

443
00:36:37,421 --> 00:36:41,920
by a two-man crew operating deep within the facility.

444
00:36:41,921 --> 00:36:45,740
A Soviet missile sent to destroy the bunker would take just over half an hour to reach

445
00:36:45,741 --> 00:36:47,360
the site.

446
00:36:47,361 --> 00:36:56,480
By that time, the 103-foot Titan II would be thundering toward its Russian target.

447
00:36:56,481 --> 00:37:02,440
Constructed in the early 60s, the Titan II missile facility is comprised of a 146-foot

448
00:37:02,441 --> 00:37:05,960
deep silo and adjoining command center.

449
00:37:05,961 --> 00:37:11,880
The structure took nearly a year and a half to build and cost over $8 million.

450
00:37:11,881 --> 00:37:16,580
Like the Cheyenne Mountain Bunker in Colorado, the Titan II facility was built to roll with

451
00:37:16,581 --> 00:37:19,620
the punches in the event of a nuclear strike.

452
00:37:19,621 --> 00:37:22,900
Everything in here is shock isolated from the missile down to the smallest piece of

453
00:37:22,901 --> 00:37:23,900
equipment.

454
00:37:23,901 --> 00:37:25,400
That means they're mounted on springs.

455
00:37:25,401 --> 00:37:29,820
So should there be a ground movement for any reason, the equipment is not going to break.

456
00:37:29,821 --> 00:37:34,120
It's just going to move around.

457
00:37:34,121 --> 00:37:40,800
The facility could also be closed off to the outside world during an attack.

458
00:37:40,801 --> 00:37:41,800
We are entering the complex.

459
00:37:41,801 --> 00:37:45,420
We've just come through an area that's very well constructed but not hardened against

460
00:37:45,421 --> 00:37:46,420
attack.

461
00:37:46,421 --> 00:37:49,740
Once we go through a series of three-ton blast doors like this one,

462
00:37:49,980 --> 00:37:52,440
we will be in the hardened part of the complex.

463
00:37:53,240 --> 00:37:57,220
There's a set of blast doors protecting the control center from the outside for us

464
00:37:57,221 --> 00:37:59,640
and another set protecting it from the silo.

465
00:38:01,480 --> 00:38:04,640
But the largest door is not inside the bunker at all.

466
00:38:05,120 --> 00:38:05,980
It's on top.

467
00:38:08,100 --> 00:38:14,300
The enormous 740-ton sliding silo door could be opened in 27 seconds.

468
00:38:14,301 --> 00:38:21,500
When closed, the door shielded the facility to help the structure survive a near hit by a nuclear weapon.

469
00:38:23,500 --> 00:38:29,440
By 1963, no less than 54 Titan II missile sites were operational throughout the country,

470
00:38:30,060 --> 00:38:32,640
ready at a moment's notice to obliterate an enemy target.

471
00:38:34,720 --> 00:38:37,120
Ironically, despite their construction and secrecy,

472
00:38:37,760 --> 00:38:41,880
nearly all the missile sites were knocked out, though not by an enemy attack.

473
00:38:44,300 --> 00:38:50,700
53 of them were dismantled as part of a mutual disarmament pact between the two superpowers.

474
00:38:53,640 --> 00:38:57,640
Today, the Arizona bunker is the last that remains in its original form.

475
00:38:58,840 --> 00:39:01,620
It now draws visitors as a national historic site

476
00:39:01,621 --> 00:39:06,120
and stands as a testament to both bunker ingenuity

477
00:39:06,121 --> 00:39:09,080
and just how close we came to nuclear war.

478
00:39:09,081 --> 00:39:16,660
next smart bombs wreak havoc in iraq

479
00:39:16,661 --> 00:39:22,320
while the underground bunker meets its high-tech end

480
00:39:22,321 --> 00:39:29,320
the titan-2 missile currently in the silo near tucson was disarmed

481
00:39:29,321 --> 00:39:32,120
drilled with holes and left above ground for one month

482
00:39:32,121 --> 00:39:35,120
to allow soviet satellites to verify its decommission

483
00:39:35,121 --> 00:39:38,520
bunkers will return on modern marvels

484
00:39:38,521 --> 00:39:45,160
The Iraq War.

485
00:39:45,720 --> 00:39:47,760
As Operation Iraqi Freedom began,

486
00:39:48,360 --> 00:39:51,240
the Iraqis entrenched their forces to defend against the onslaught

487
00:39:51,241 --> 00:39:53,320
of U.S.-led coalition troops.

488
00:39:54,380 --> 00:39:55,860
Just as in Desert Storm,

489
00:39:56,540 --> 00:39:58,720
bunkers and fortified structures housed everything

490
00:39:58,721 --> 00:40:01,680
from artillery to Saddam Hussein himself.

491
00:40:02,340 --> 00:40:04,820
But the sense of security provided by these bunkers

492
00:40:04,821 --> 00:40:06,120
was quickly shattered.

493
00:40:08,520 --> 00:40:22,280
Smart bombs targeted and struck even the most secret of Saddam's hidden bunkers.

494
00:40:23,180 --> 00:40:27,560
Nothing could have prepared the Iraqi troops for the state-of-the-art precision-guided weapons.

495
00:40:29,640 --> 00:40:34,160
The war with Iraq suggested bunkers are no longer viable military options

496
00:40:34,161 --> 00:40:37,700
when fighting well-organized and technologically advanced militaries.

497
00:40:38,520 --> 00:40:43,900
And the U.S. Armament Center at Eglin Air Force Base wants to make that a reality.

498
00:40:47,580 --> 00:40:50,360
Occupying an area two-thirds the size of Rhode Island,

499
00:40:51,020 --> 00:40:56,520
the Air Armament Center on the Florida Panhandle builds and tests state-of-the-art bunker-busting weapons.

500
00:40:59,320 --> 00:41:04,420
Here, test pilots and scientists are able to perfect the guidance and delivery systems for these bombs

501
00:41:04,421 --> 00:41:07,480
by dropping them as if in a real battle situation.

502
00:41:08,520 --> 00:41:15,100
When we're testing bunker buster type weapons, the idea is to put the weapon on the target.

503
00:41:15,480 --> 00:41:22,300
Ultimately, you plan a specific route to get to the target and you release the weapon at a specified range from the target.

504
00:41:24,080 --> 00:41:30,540
The weapons are so accurate, they can be dropped by planes up to 10 miles away and hit within feet of their target.

505
00:41:30,541 --> 00:41:37,240
Special fuses, guidance systems, and the ability to burrow underground

506
00:41:37,241 --> 00:41:39,540
make these bombs perfect bunker killers.

507
00:41:41,540 --> 00:41:44,320
But how do you test the individual components of a weapon

508
00:41:44,321 --> 00:41:46,460
that travels over a thousand feet per second

509
00:41:46,461 --> 00:41:50,260
and is vaporized within milliseconds after impact?

510
00:41:51,940 --> 00:41:54,240
We use our sled track tests.

511
00:41:54,241 --> 00:41:57,360
Three, two, one.

512
00:42:00,540 --> 00:42:10,280
These sled tracks are like a railroad track going down for a long distance.

513
00:42:11,020 --> 00:42:14,960
We put rocket motors behind an actual bomb,

514
00:42:15,620 --> 00:42:19,820
and we have these things propelled down at the exact same speed

515
00:42:19,821 --> 00:42:21,580
that they would see when they hit the ground.

516
00:42:22,500 --> 00:42:27,120
At the end of the sled track, we build an actual bunker.

517
00:42:27,121 --> 00:42:31,540
and we'll build it with the protection that we normally see in the ground.

518
00:42:32,160 --> 00:42:35,180
We have the hardened concrete, we have layers of dirt,

519
00:42:35,580 --> 00:42:39,440
and with that what you can see is that we can test any of our warheads

520
00:42:39,441 --> 00:42:42,380
very effectively just like it were flight tested.

521
00:42:49,960 --> 00:42:52,880
High-speed cameras and electronic test equipment

522
00:42:52,881 --> 00:42:56,480
catch the apocalyptic destruction unleashed in a fraction of a second.

523
00:42:57,120 --> 00:43:08,420
I do see a time when bunkers will no longer be a utility military protection

524
00:43:08,421 --> 00:43:14,080
because we do have much more capable weapons in our inventory.

525
00:43:14,081 --> 00:43:27,980
Despite their practical limitations, bunkers still continue to be built.

526
00:43:28,960 --> 00:43:33,520
Humans will always have an instinctual need to be protected and taken out of harm's way.

527
00:43:36,880 --> 00:43:40,760
The bunker mentality is as old as nearly any thought process.

528
00:43:40,761 --> 00:43:44,660
and as long as this persists

529
00:43:44,661 --> 00:43:46,260
presidents, dictators

530
00:43:46,261 --> 00:43:47,640
and common people alike

531
00:43:47,641 --> 00:43:50,000
will quietly dig into the earth

532
00:43:50,001 --> 00:43:51,640
in search of a degree of protection

533
00:43:51,641 --> 00:43:54,100
that may no longer

534
00:43:54,101 --> 00:43:54,980
be obtainable

